registerChapter({
  id: 'local-compose',
  num: '26',
  phase: 'Phase 5 · Locality and the frame rule',
  title: 'Locality composes',
  blurb: 'Sequencing, conditionals and loops preserve locality, so the frame rule will apply to programs rather than to single commands. The sequencing proof is where Unit 24&rsquo;s extra conjunct is finally spent, and the loop proof needs an induction the language will not let you write directly.',

  orient: {
    youWill: [
      "Prove that <code>c₁ ;; c₂</code> is local whenever <code>c₁</code> and <code>c₂</code> are, and point at the argument position where Unit 24&rsquo;s extra conjunct is consumed.",
      "Watch that proof fail under Unit 24&rsquo;s weaker definition, stranded on the goal <code>⊢ sMid.heap.disjoint hFrame</code> with nothing in the context that yields it.",
      "Name a state Lean introduced inaccessibly, with <code>rename_i</code>, and say why <code>| seq sMid hex₁ hex₂ =&gt;</code> is refused.",
      "Close the gap between <code>Exec c₂ ⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩ r₂</code> and <code>Exec c₂ r₁ r₂</code> with two backwards rewrites and structure eta.",
      "Prove locality of <code>ite</code> in eight lines, and say why the guard crosses from the small heap to the big one with no argument at all.",
      "Read <code>Invalid target: Index in target&rsquo;s type is not a variable</code> and repair it with the constant-command idiom: a variable command, an equation carried as a premise, and eight branches killed by constructor no-confusion.",
      "Prove locality of <code>loop</code> by induction on the derivation, and say which hypothesis had to be left under the quantifier for the induction hypothesis to be usable."
    ],
    needs: [
      "Unit 24: <code>HeapLocal</code>, its five components, the opening <code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code>, and the weaker <code>HeapLocalWeak</code> that came before it.",
      "Unit 19: <code>Exec.seq</code>, <code>Exec.iteTrue</code>, <code>Exec.iteFalse</code>, <code>Exec.loopFalse</code>, <code>Exec.loopTrue</code>; inversion with <code>cases … with | ctor …</code>; <code>rename_i</code> and the daggered inaccessible name.",
      "Unit 20: induction over a derivation, reading the induction hypothesis off the goal display, and <code>cases</code> on an equation between constructors.",
      "Unit 18: <code>State</code> as a structure with exactly two fields, and <code>BExpr.eval</code> taking a <code>Store</code> rather than a <code>State</code>."
    ],
    payoff: "After this page every one of the eight ways to build a command is known to be local, so the frame rule proved in Unit 27 applies to any program in the language and not only to the five that touch memory directly. The loop half is also the manoeuvre Unit 36 needs, where the same idiom is compulsory and the theorem is harder."
  },

  blocks: [

    /* ================================================= the composition === */

    {t:'p', h:"Sequencing, then. A run of <code>c₁ ;; c₂</code> goes through a middle state, and that middle state is the whole difficulty. Locality of <code>c₁</code> says something about running <code>c₁</code> on a bigger heap; locality of <code>c₂</code> says something about running <code>c₂</code> on a bigger heap; and nothing so far connects the heap the first run leaves behind to the heap the second run is entitled to be started on."},

    {t:'p', h:"Take the two hypotheses and apply them in order. Locality of <code>c₁</code>, at the frame <code>hFrame</code>, applied to the first half of the run, hands back two things: that the middle heap is still disjoint from the frame, and a run of <code>c₁</code> on the combined heap ending somewhere whose store and heap are the middle state&rsquo;s, with the frame put back beside it. Now look at what locality of <code>c₂</code> demands before it will say anything at all. It demands a heap, a frame, and a proof that the two are disjoint. The heap is the middle heap. The frame is the same frame. The proof is the first thing the previous application returned."},

    {t:'steps', title:'The composition, with no Lean in it',
     items:[
       {k:'Split the run', h:"<code>Exec (c₁ ;; c₂) ⟨σ, h⟩ s'</code> holds for exactly one reason: there is a state <code>sMid</code>, a run of <code>c₁</code> from <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to it, and a run of <code>c₂</code> from it to <code>s'</code>."},
       {k:'Spend the first hypothesis', h:"Locality of <code>c₁</code> at the first run and the frame <code>hFrame</code> returns <code>Heap.disjoint sMid.heap hFrame</code>, and a state <code>r₁</code> with a big run of <code>c₁</code> reaching it, <code>r₁.store = sMid.store</code> and <code>r₁.heap = Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame</code>."},
       {k:'Spend the second', h:"Locality of <code>c₂</code>, applied to the second run with <code>sMid.heap</code> as the small heap. That application is legal only because step 2 produced the disjointness it asks for. It returns <code>Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame</code> — which is the first half of what you have to prove — and a state <code>r₂</code> reached by running <code>c₂</code> from <code>⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩</code>, with the two equations about <code>s'</code>."},
       {k:'Glue the two big runs', h:"<code>Exec.seq</code> joins a run of <code>c₁</code> to a run of <code>c₂</code> provided the state where the first ends is the state where the second begins. The first ends at <code>r₁</code>. The second begins at <code>⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩</code>. Step 2 says those two states have the same store and the same heap, which is not yet the same as saying they are the same state."},
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"Step 3 is the sentence this module has been working towards. Unit 24 added the disjointness conjunct to <code>HeapLocal</code> by starting the frame proof with the textbook definition and reading the missing fact off a goal that would not close; Unit 25 established it twice more, once for nothing — erasing a cell can only shrink a domain, so <code>free</code> keeps disjointness whatever it was handed — and once as the half of the <code>write</code> proof where the disjointness hypothesis is spent. In all five of those the conjunct is a thing to establish, produced at the end and handed to a caller. Here it is a thing to use: it is the fifth argument of the second application, and without it that application cannot be written down at all."},

    {t:'p', h:"That claim is checkable, because Unit 24&rsquo;s weaker definition is still in the corpus. <code>HeapLocalWeak</code> is <code>HeapLocal</code> with the disjointness conclusion deleted. Try the same four steps with it."},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"The composition attempted with the weak definition. The <code>?_</code> stands where the disjointness proof would go, and the <code>sorry</code> stands for the rest, which is not the problem.",
     src:"theorem heapLocalWeak_seq_stuck {c₁ c₂ : Cmd}\n    (h₁ : HeapLocalWeak c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocalWeak c₂) :\n    HeapLocalWeak (c₁ ;; c₂) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | seq hex₁ hex₂ =>\n      rename_i sMid\n      obtain ⟨r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame sMid hd hex₁\n      obtain ⟨r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := h₂ sMid.store sMid.heap hFrame s' ?_ hex₂\n      sorry"},

    {t:'state', cap:"What Lean reports, trimmed to the last four lines of a fifteen-line context; the fold below has all of it. The <code>snippet:L:C:</code> prefix is dropped, which is the convention for every message and every goal display on this page.",
     src:"error: don't know how to synthesize placeholder\ncontext:\nhr₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r₁\nhst₁ : r₁.store = sMid.store\nhhp₁ : r₁.heap = sMid.heap.union hFrame\n⊢ sMid.heap.disjoint hFrame"},

    {t:'p', h:"The stranded goal is the conjunct itself. The context holds the disjointness of <code>h</code> from the frame, the two halves of the run, and everything the first application produced — and none of those says anything about the middle heap&rsquo;s relationship to the frame. The first command ran, the heap changed, and the weak definition threw away the only fact that survives that change. Sequencing is where a definition that describes a single command in isolation stops being enough."},

    {t:'detail', title:'The whole context at the stranded goal', tag:'aside', open:false,
     blocks:[
       {t:'p', h:"Fifteen hypotheses, so that the claim above can be checked rather than taken on trust. Read down the list asking of each line whether it mentions <code>sMid.heap</code> and <code>hFrame</code> together."},
       {t:'state', cap:"The message in full.",
        src:"error: don't know how to synthesize placeholder\ncontext:\nc₁ c₂ : Cmd\nh₁ : HeapLocalWeak c₁\nh₂ : HeapLocalWeak c₂\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\ns' : State\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nsMid : State\nhex₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h } sMid\nhex₂ : Exec c₂ sMid s'\nr₁ : State\nhr₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r₁\nhst₁ : r₁.store = sMid.store\nhhp₁ : r₁.heap = sMid.heap.union hFrame\n⊢ sMid.heap.disjoint hFrame"},
       {t:'p', h:"Two lines mention both. <code>hd</code> is about <code>h</code>, the heap before <code>c₁</code> ran. <code>hhp₁</code> is about <code>r₁.heap</code>, which is <code>sMid.heap</code> and the frame already combined — a heap in which the two are no longer separable, since <code>Heap.union</code> is total and forgets which side each cell came from. Neither is the goal, and no combination of them is."}
     ]},

    /* ===================================================== the proof === */

    {t:'sec', s:'The state Lean will not let you name'},

    {t:'p', h:"With the strong definition the four steps go through, and the Lean is a transcript of them. It opens the way every locality proof in this module opens: <code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code>, then an inversion. <code>Exec (c₁ ;; c₂)</code> has one rule, so the inversion produces one branch."},

    {t:'p', h:"The premises of <code>Exec.seq</code> are two runs, and the alternative can name them. The middle state is a different matter. The rule was declared as <code>| seq {s s' s'' c₁ c₂} (h₁ : Exec c₁ s s') (h₂ : Exec c₂ s' s'')</code>, concluding <code>Exec (c₁ ;; c₂) s s''</code>. The middle state is an implicit binder, not a premise, so it is not among the things the alternative&rsquo;s name list refers to. Asking for three names says you have miscounted the premises."},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"Naming the middle state where the premises go.",
     src:"  cases hex with\n  | seq sMid hex₁ hex₂ => sorry"},

    {t:'state', cap:"Lean counts premises, and there are two.",
     src:"error: Too many variable names provided at alternative `seq`: 3 provided, but 2 expected"},

    {t:'p', h:"The middle state does arrive — it has to, since both premises mention it — but it arrives inaccessible, as <code>s'✝</code>. Unit 19 introduced <code>rename_i</code> and showed it naming the middle state of a <code>seq</code>, in an aside where nothing depended on it. Here something does. The proof below ends by <i>stating</i> an equation — that the state the big run of <code>c₁</code> reaches is the middle state&rsquo;s two fields with the frame beside them — and a <code>have</code> has to spell out what it is proving. With the middle state unnameable there is nothing to write on the right-hand side. <code>rename_i sMid</code> takes the last inaccessible entry in the context and rebinds it, and from that line on the middle state can be written into a type."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:"Thirteen lines, and the last four of them are all one problem, which the next section is about.",
     src:"theorem heapLocal_seq {c₁ c₂ : Cmd} (h₁ : HeapLocal c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocal c₂) :\n    HeapLocal (c₁ ;; c₂) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | seq hex₁ hex₂ =>\n      rename_i sMid\n      obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame sMid hd hex₁\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := h₂ sMid.store sMid.heap hFrame s' hdMid hex₂\n      refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩\n      have heq : r₁ = ⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩ := by\n        rw [← hst₁, ← hhp₁]\n      rw [heq]\n      exact hr₂"},

    {t:'trace', title:'heapLocal_seq, step by step',
     start:'after intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex',
     steps:[
       {tac:'cases hex with | seq hex₁ hex₂ =>',
        state:"case seq\ns'✝ : State\nhex₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h } s'✝\nhex₂ : Exec c₂ s'✝ s'\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r, Exec (c₁ ;; c₂) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧ r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame",
        h:"Seven context lines above <code>s'✝</code> are omitted here and in every step below, and none of them changes: <code>c₁ c₂</code>, <code>h₁</code>, <code>h₂</code>, <code>σ</code>, <code>h hFrame</code>, <code>s'</code> and <code>hd</code>. The goal is <code>HeapLocal</code>&rsquo;s conjunction with the command instantiated, and the inversion has not touched it: unlike <code>write</code> or <code>free</code>, the <code>seq</code> rule says nothing about what the final state looks like, so <code>s'</code> stays an arbitrary state throughout."},
       {tac:'rename_i sMid',
        state:"case seq\nsMid : State\nhex₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h } sMid\nhex₂ : Exec c₂ sMid s'",
        h:"The last inaccessible name in the context becomes <code>sMid</code>, and every hypothesis mentioning it is redisplayed. Nothing else moved, so the goal is left out of this display and the two below."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame sMid hd hex₁',
        state:"hdMid : sMid.heap.disjoint hFrame\nr₁ : State\nhr₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r₁\nhst₁ : r₁.store = sMid.store\nhhp₁ : r₁.heap = sMid.heap.union hFrame",
        h:"Five new hypotheses, one per component of <code>HeapLocal</code>&rsquo;s conclusion. <code>hdMid</code> is the conjunct. <code>r₁</code> is the state the big run of <code>c₁</code> reaches, and the last two lines are all anyone will ever know about it."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := h₂ sMid.store sMid.heap hFrame s\' hdMid hex₂',
        state:"hdEnd : s'.heap.disjoint hFrame\nr₂ : State\nhr₂ : Exec c₂ { store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame } r₂\nhst₂ : r₂.store = s'.store\nhhp₂ : r₂.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame",
        h:"<code>hdMid</code> is in the fifth argument position, which is where the disjointness hypothesis of <code>HeapLocal</code> goes. The three hypotheses this produces are three of the five components the goal wants, exactly as they stand. What it does not produce is a run starting at <code>r₁</code>."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩',
        state:"case seq\n⊢ Exec c₂ r₁ r₂",
        h:"Four components placed, one hole left. <code>Exec.seq hr₁ ?_</code> fixes the first half of the big run to be <code>hr₁</code>, and that decides where the second half has to start: at <code>r₁</code>, the state <code>hr₁</code> ends in."},
       {tac:'rw [heq]',
        state:"case seq\n⊢ Exec c₂ { store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame } r₂",
        h:"With <code>heq : r₁ = ⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩</code> in hand, rewriting replaces <code>r₁</code> in the goal. What is left is <code>hr₂</code>&rsquo;s type, character for character."}
     ],
     done:'No goals.'},

    /* ================================================== structure eta === */

    {t:'sec', s:'Two equations are a state'},

    {t:'p', h:"The gap the last two steps close is the one place in this proof where the mathematics and the Lean come apart. Informally, step 4 of the sketch is finished: the second run starts where the first ends. Formally, the hole has type <code>Exec c₂ r₁ r₂</code> and the hypothesis has type <code>Exec c₂ ⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩ r₂</code>, and those are two different states as far as the context is concerned. What locality of <code>c₁</code> handed back was <i>a</i> state together with two equations about its fields. It never handed back the state itself."},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"The last four lines replaced by the term that ought to work.",
     src:"      exact ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩"},

    {t:'state', cap:"The two states, side by side, in a complaint about an argument.",
     src:"error: Application type mismatch: The argument\n  hr₂\nhas type\n  Exec c₂ { store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame } r₂\nbut is expected to have type\n  Exec c₂ r₁ r₂\nin the application\n  Exec.seq hr₁ hr₂"},

    {t:'p', h:"The repair rests on something about <code>State</code> that Unit 18 declared and nothing has needed until now. A <code>State</code> is a structure with two fields and no others, so a state and the pair of its own fields are the same term — not equal by some theorem, but interchangeable by definition. That is structure eta."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"No tactic, no lemma, no hypothesis. Both sides reduce to the same thing, which is what <code>rfl</code> checks.",
     src:"example (s : State) : s = ⟨s.store, s.heap⟩ := rfl"},

    {t:'p', h:"So knowing both fields of <code>r₁</code> is knowing <code>r₁</code>. The two equations <code>hst₁</code> and <code>hhp₁</code> are exactly that knowledge, and turning them into the equation the goal wants takes them in the direction they were not written in."},

    {t:'trace', title:'The have, with its rewrite split in two',
     start:'⊢ r₁ = { store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame }',
     steps:[
       {tac:'rw [← hst₁]',
        state:"⊢ r₁ = { store := r₁.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame }",
        h:"<code>hst₁ : r₁.store = sMid.store</code> read forwards would replace <code>r₁.store</code> by <code>sMid.store</code>, and there is no <code>r₁.store</code> in the goal to replace. Read backwards it does the useful thing: every <code>sMid.store</code> in the goal becomes <code>r₁.store</code>. The first field of the right-hand side is now <code>r₁</code>&rsquo;s own."},
       {tac:'rw [← hhp₁]',
        state:"No goals.",
        h:"The same move on the heap. The right-hand side becomes <code>{ store := r₁.store, heap := r₁.heap }</code>, which is <code>r₁</code> by eta, so the goal has become <code>r₁ = r₁</code> and the <code>rfl</code> that <code>rw</code> attempts after every rewrite closes it. Nothing on the screen ever shows that intermediate form; the goal is gone before it can be printed."}
     ],
     done:'No goals.'},

    {t:'p', h:"Eta is at work once more in this proof, silently. <code>h₂</code> is applied at <code>sMid.store</code> and <code>sMid.heap</code>, so it demands a run starting at <code>⟨sMid.store, sMid.heap⟩</code> — and <code>hex₂</code> is a run starting at <code>sMid</code>. That is accepted with no line of proof, because an application compares types up to reduction. The place where the same identity has to be spelled out is the place where the two states are <i>different terms</i> whose equality is a fact about the context rather than about the definition of <code>State</code>: <code>r₁</code> reduces to nothing in particular, and only <code>hst₁</code> and <code>hhp₁</code> say what its fields are."},

    /* =================================================== exercise m8-4 === */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'m8-4',
     name:'heapLocal_seq',
     hard:false,
     why:"The first theorem in the course whose hypotheses are themselves locality statements, and the one that turns locality from a property of five particular commands into a property closed under program construction. Unit 27 proves the frame rule from <code>HeapLocal c</code> for an arbitrary <code>c</code>; that theorem is worth having only because this one, together with the two below it, makes the hypothesis dischargeable for every program in the language. It is also the first place in the corpus where Unit 24&rsquo;s extra conjunct is consumed rather than produced; the loop proof at the foot of this page does it a second time, feeding the same conjunct to an induction hypothesis. Unit 27 receives it as well, but puts it into a <code>∗</code> rather than using it to license an application, so this is the shape of argument the conjunct was invented for.",
     setup:"The whole argument is the four-step sketch above, and the four steps map onto the Lean one to one. The two hypotheses are <code>h₁ : HeapLocal c₁</code> and <code>h₂ : HeapLocal c₂</code>, which are <code>∀</code>-statements you apply like functions: six arguments each, in the order <code>σ</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>hFrame</code>, <code>s'</code>, the disjointness, the run. The middle state arrives inaccessible and <code>rename_i</code> is how you get a name for it. The last obligation is the one the section above is about.",
     goal:"theorem heapLocal_seq {c₁ c₂ : Cmd} (h₁ : HeapLocal c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocal c₂) :\n    HeapLocal (c₁ ;; c₂) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded: you are given a frame disjoint from <code>h</code> and a run of <code>c₁ ;; c₂</code> from <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to <code>s'</code>, and you must produce that <code>s'.heap</code> is disjoint from the frame, plus a state <code>r</code> reached by running <code>c₁ ;; c₂</code> from <code>⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩</code> whose store is <code>s'.store</code> and whose heap is <code>Heap.union s'.heap hFrame</code>. The two hypotheses say the same thing about <code>c₁</code> and about <code>c₂</code> separately.",
       "A run of the sequence is a run of <code>c₁</code> to some middle state followed by a run of <code>c₂</code> from that middle state. Take the two halves in order. Locality of <code>c₁</code>, at your frame, says the middle heap is still disjoint from the frame and hands you a run of <code>c₁</code> on the combined heap; that first fact is exactly the disjointness locality of <code>c₂</code> demands before it will say anything, so the second half can be lifted too. Three of the five things you owe are then what the second lifting returned, and the fourth is the two lifted runs put back together as a single run of the sequence.",
       "<code>cases hex with | seq hex₁ hex₂ =&gt;</code>, then <code>rename_i</code> for the middle state. Both applications are ordinary function application, and their results come apart with <code>obtain</code> into five names each. Assemble with <code>refine</code>, leaving the second half of the big run as a hole. That hole needs an equation between two states; get it from the two field equations the first application returned, with <code>rw</code> in the backwards direction, and finish with <code>rw</code> at the goal.",
       "<code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code>, then <code>cases hex with | seq hex₁ hex₂ =&gt;</code>, then <code>rename_i sMid</code> — which leaves <code>hex₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h } sMid</code> and <code>hex₂ : Exec c₂ sMid s'</code>. The two applications are <code>h₁ σ h hFrame sMid hd hex₁</code> and <code>h₂ sMid.store sMid.heap hFrame s' hdMid hex₂</code>, each destructured by <code>obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ :=</code> and <code>obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ :=</code>. Then <code>refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩</code> leaves <code>⊢ Exec c₂ r₁ r₂</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem heapLocal_seq {c₁ c₂ : Cmd} (h₁ : HeapLocal c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocal c₂) :\n    HeapLocal (c₁ ;; c₂) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | seq hex₁ hex₂ =>\n      rename_i sMid\n      obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame sMid hd hex₁\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := h₂ sMid.store sMid.heap hFrame s' hdMid hex₂\n      refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩\n      have heq : r₁ = ⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩ := by\n        rw [← hst₁, ← hhp₁]\n      rw [heq]\n      exact hr₂",
     solNote:"Thirteen lines, of which four exist because a state and the pair of its fields are the same object and the context knows the fields rather than the state. If you climbed all four hints and are still stuck, open this and read it against the four-step sketch: lines 3&ndash;6 are step 1, line 7 is step 2, line 8 is step 3, and lines 9&ndash;13 are step 4.",
     expl:"Two applications and a join. The first application spends the disjointness you were given and produces the disjointness of the middle heap; the second spends that and produces the disjointness of the final heap, which is the first component of the answer. The two big runs are joined by <code>Exec.seq</code>, whose only demand is that the first end where the second begins — and meeting that demand is the whole of the last four lines, because the first application described its state by its fields rather than handing it over.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex', h:"Discharged the four binders and the two implications of <code>HeapLocal</code>. The goal is the conjunction, with <code>s'</code> an arbitrary state."},
       {tac:'cases hex with | seq hex₁ hex₂ =>', h:"Inversion. One rule concludes <code>Exec (c₁ ;; c₂) _ _</code>, so one branch, and in it the run is two runs through a state Lean introduced but did not name. The goal is unchanged, because <code>Exec.seq</code>&rsquo;s conclusion says nothing about the final state."},
       {tac:'rename_i sMid', h:"Gave the middle state a name. Nothing was proved; a name that could not be typed became one that can, and the two half-runs are redisplayed in terms of it."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame sMid hd hex₁', h:"Applied locality of <code>c₁</code> and took the result apart into its five components. <code>hdMid</code> is the extra conjunct, and it is the only reason the next line is legal."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := h₂ sMid.store sMid.heap hFrame s\' hdMid hex₂', h:"Applied locality of <code>c₂</code> with the middle state&rsquo;s two fields as the store and the heap. <code>hex₂</code> is a run starting at <code>sMid</code>, and what is wanted is a run starting at <code>⟨sMid.store, sMid.heap⟩</code>; eta makes those the same type and no line is needed for it."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩', h:"Placed four of the five components. <code>hdEnd</code>, <code>hst₂</code> and <code>hhp₂</code> are used unchanged; the witness state is <code>r₂</code>, the end of the second big run. The hole is the second half of <code>Exec.seq</code>, and its type is <code>Exec c₂ r₁ r₂</code> — the start is forced by <code>hr₁</code>."},
       {tac:'have heq : r₁ = ⟨sMid.store, Heap.union sMid.heap hFrame⟩ := by', h:"Stated the identification the hole needs, as an equation between states. It is not available as a hypothesis; the first application returned a state and two facts about its fields."},
       {tac:'rw [← hst₁, ← hhp₁]', h:"Turned the right-hand side into <code>⟨r₁.store, r₁.heap⟩</code>, one field at a time, by reading both equations backwards. Structure eta makes that term <code>r₁</code>, so the goal became <code>r₁ = r₁</code> and <code>rw</code>&rsquo;s trailing <code>rfl</code> closed it."},
       {tac:'rw [heq]', h:"Replaced <code>r₁</code> in the goal. <code>⊢ Exec c₂ r₁ r₂</code> became <code>⊢ Exec c₂ { store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame } r₂</code>, which is <code>hr₂</code>&rsquo;s type character for character."},
       {tac:'exact hr₂', h:"The goal is now the type of a hypothesis."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'Everything in scope when the hole opens',
        start:"after the two applications, with the goal still the five-component conjunction",
        steps:[
          {tac:'refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩',
           state:"case seq\nc₁ c₂ : Cmd\nh₁ : HeapLocal c₁\nh₂ : HeapLocal c₂\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\ns' : State\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nsMid : State\nhex₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h } sMid\nhex₂ : Exec c₂ sMid s'\nhdMid : sMid.heap.disjoint hFrame\nr₁ : State\nhr₁ : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r₁\nhst₁ : r₁.store = sMid.store\nhhp₁ : r₁.heap = sMid.heap.union hFrame\nhdEnd : s'.heap.disjoint hFrame\nr₂ : State\nhr₂ : Exec c₂ { store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame } r₂\nhst₂ : r₂.store = s'.store\nhhp₂ : r₂.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame\n⊢ Exec c₂ r₁ r₂",
           h:"Nothing trimmed this time; the main-line trace omitted seven of these lines at every step. Read down it asking which hypothesis could prove <code>Exec c₂ r₁ r₂</code>. <code>hex₁</code> and <code>hr₁</code> are runs of <code>c₁</code>. <code>hex₂</code> is a run of <code>c₂</code>, but on the small heap. <code>hd</code>, <code>hdMid</code> and <code>hdEnd</code> are disjointness facts, and <code>hst₁</code>, <code>hhp₁</code>, <code>hst₂</code>, <code>hhp₂</code> are equations between fields. One line is left: <code>hr₂</code> is a run of <code>c₂</code>, on the right heap, ending at the right state. The only thing wrong with it is how its starting state is written."},
          {tac:'rw [heq]',
           state:"case seq\n⊢ Exec c₂ { store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame } r₂",
           h:"After the <code>have</code>. The goal is now <code>hr₂</code>&rsquo;s type, character for character, so <code>exact hr₂</code> ends it."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'},
       {t:'detail', title:'The same theorem with the middle state never named', tag:'aside', open:false,
        blocks:[
          {t:'p', h:"<code>rename_i</code> is what lets this proof <i>state</i> things about the middle state. It is not what lets it <i>reach</i> the state. In both applications the store and the heap can be left as underscores, and Lean solves <code>⟨?σ, ?h⟩</code> against <code>s'✝</code> — by the same structure eta the section above is about. What has no name then is the equation, so there is no <code>have</code> to write; the two backwards rewrites are aimed at <code>hr₂</code> instead, and <code>Exec.seq</code> takes the result because <code>⟨r₁.store, r₁.heap⟩</code> <i>is</i> <code>r₁</code>."},
          {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"Nine lines, no <code>rename_i</code>, no <code>have</code>, no <code>refine</code>.",
           src:"theorem heapLocal_seq_unnamed {c₁ c₂ : Cmd} (h₁ : HeapLocal c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocal c₂) :\n    HeapLocal (c₁ ;; c₂) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | seq hex₁ hex₂ =>\n      obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame _ hd hex₁\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := h₂ _ _ hFrame s' hdMid hex₂\n      rw [← hst₁, ← hhp₁] at hr₂\n      exact ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.seq hr₁ hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩"},
          {t:'p', h:"Four lines shorter and the version not to write. Every one of the underscores is a state the reader cannot see, the rewrite changes a hypothesis behind them rather than the goal in front of them, and the three facts about the middle heap are now spread across two hypotheses that never mention it by name. In the loop proof the same style has three states arriving daggered at once, with <code>s</code> and <code>s'</code> from the outer <code>intro</code> still in scope under almost the same spellings — which is where it stops being readable at all."}
        ]},
       {t:'p', h:"One thing this proof never does is look at <code>c₁</code> or <code>c₂</code>. They are arbitrary commands and the only facts used about them are the two hypotheses, which is what makes the theorem a closure property rather than a calculation. The same is true of the two below it, and it is why adding a ninth constructor to <code>Cmd</code> tomorrow would require exactly one new locality proof and no revision of these three."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Reaching for <code>obtain ⟨sMid, hex₁, hex₂⟩ := hex</code> instead of <code>cases</code>. It is the natural move — <code>Exec.seq</code>&rsquo;s content is a middle state and two runs, which reads like a three-part package — and Lean accepts the line without a word. What it does is put all three into the context <i>inaccessibly</i>, as <code>s'✝</code>, <code>h₁✝</code> and <code>h₂✝</code>, because those are the names <code>Exec.seq</code> itself gives its arguments and an anonymous-constructor pattern on an inductive hypothesis does not override them. The complaint arrives on the next line, in the wrong place and about the wrong thing: <code>Unknown identifier `sMid`</code> and <code>Unknown identifier `hex₁`</code>, pointing at the application of <code>h₁</code>. Worse, the daggered <code>h₁✝</code> and <code>h₂✝</code> print next to the real <code>h₁</code> and <code>h₂</code>, the two locality hypotheses, so the context looks as though it holds four things with two names. Use <code>cases … with | seq hex₁ hex₂ =&gt;</code>, which names the premises, and <code>rename_i</code> for the state.",
     variants:"Weaken <code>HeapLocal</code> to Unit 24&rsquo;s <code>HeapLocalWeak</code> — same statement with the disjointness conjunct deleted from the conclusion — and this theorem is no longer provable by this argument: the second application asks for <code>Heap.disjoint sMid.heap hFrame</code> and the context, shown in full above, does not contain it or anything that yields it. &middot; Apply the second hypothesis at <code>σ</code> and <code>h</code> rather than at the middle state&rsquo;s fields and the run <code>hex₂</code> is rejected: <code>Application type mismatch</code>, <code>hex₂</code> has type <code>Exec c₂ sMid s'</code> but is expected to have type <code>Exec c₂ { store := σ, heap := h } s'</code>. The middle state is where the second command begins, and that is the only place it can begin. &middot; Drop the <code>have</code> and write <code>Exec.seq hr₁ hr₂</code> directly and the error is an <code>Application type mismatch</code> on <code>hr₂</code>, quoting the two states in full — <code>{ store := sMid.store, heap := sMid.heap.union hFrame }</code> against <code>r₁</code>. The fix is not a stronger tactic; it is the observation that the context describes <code>r₁</code> by its fields and a state is its fields. &middot; The one deletion that does <i>not</i> break these thirteen lines is instructive. Delete the disjointness <i>hypothesis</i> from <code>HeapLocal</code> and keep the conjunct, and the proof still compiles with <code>hd</code> struck from the <code>intro</code> and from both applications — which reads like a refutation of everything above, until you ask what the weakened property is worth. <code>heapLocal_skip</code> proved its conjunct <i>by handing over</i> <code>hd</code>; without the hypothesis that proof stops at <code>don't know how to synthesize placeholder for argument `left`</code> on <code>⊢ { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.disjoint hFrame</code>, and <code>skip</code> genuinely fails the weakened property — one cell owned, the same cell framed, and the final heap is not disjoint from the frame. So the closure theorem survives while the thing it closes over becomes unsatisfiable. What sequencing spends is the conjunct in the <i>conclusion</i>, and that is the one that cannot go."
    },

    /* ================================================== conditionals === */

    {t:'sec', s:'Conditionals, where nothing happens'},

    {t:'p', h:"A conditional is two rules, each with a guard premise and one sub-run, and each concluding a run of <code>.ite b c₁ c₂</code>. Inversion gives two branches; in each, the sub-run is a run of one of the two commands from the same starting state to the same finishing state as the whole conditional. So each branch is one application of the matching hypothesis and one rebuild, with nothing in between."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:"Eight lines of proof, and the two branches differ only in which hypothesis and which rule they name.",
     src:"theorem heapLocal_ite {b : BExpr} {c₁ c₂ : Cmd}\n    (h₁ : HeapLocal c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocal c₂) : HeapLocal (.ite b c₁ c₂) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | iteTrue hb hex' =>\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame s' hd hex'\n      exact ⟨hdEnd, r, Exec.iteTrue hb hr, hst, hhp⟩\n  | iteFalse hb hex' =>\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₂ σ h hFrame s' hd hex'\n      exact ⟨hdEnd, r, Exec.iteFalse hb hr, hst, hhp⟩"},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>hb</code> is where the content of those eight lines is, and the content is that nothing is done to it. Inversion produced it as a fact about the small run: <code>hb : BExpr.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store b = true</code>. <code>Exec.iteTrue</code>, applied to build the big run, wants a fact about the big one: <code>BExpr.eval { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame }.store b = true</code>. Those are different terms. They are the same type, because the projection of a structure literal reduces to the field, and both reduce to <code>BExpr.eval σ b = true</code>."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"The transfer as a term. The proof is the hypothesis, with nothing done to it.",
     src:"example (σ : Store) (h hFrame : Heap) (b : BExpr)\n    (hb : b.eval (⟨σ, h⟩ : State).store = true) :\n    b.eval (⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩ : State).store = true := hb"},

    {t:'p', h:"That is not a fact about conditionals. It is a fact about <i>this language&rsquo;s</i> guards, and it was decided in Unit 18 by giving <code>BExpr.eval</code> the type <code>Store → BExpr → Bool</code> rather than <code>State → BExpr → Bool</code>. A guard that could read memory would not survive the addition of a frame, because a frame adds cells and a heap-reading test can notice."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"A guard that asks whether address 0 is allocated. The same store, the same small heap, and one extra cell in a frame that the program does not own: the answer flips.",
     src:"def guardAllocated (s : State) : Bool :=\n  match s.heap 0 with\n  | none   => false\n  | some _ => true\n\nexample : guardAllocated ⟨fun _ => 0, Heap.empty⟩ = false := by\n  simp [guardAllocated, Heap.empty]\n\nexample :\n    guardAllocated ⟨fun _ => 0, Heap.union Heap.empty (Heap.singleton 0 7)⟩ = true := by\n  simp [guardAllocated, Heap.union, Heap.empty, Heap.singleton]"},

    {t:'p', h:"Put that test in a conditional and the two branches of the program taken under the small heap and under the big heap are different branches, so the final states have no reason to agree and locality fails at its first clause. The eight lines above are short because that decision, taken for reasons that had nothing to do with the frame rule, happens to be exactly what the frame rule needs. Unit 38 returns to what else a command may do and still be local; allocation is the interesting case, and it is not the only one."},

    /* =================================================== exercise x52 === */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x52',
     name:'heapLocal_ite',
     hard:false,
     why:"The cheap one of the three, and it is here for two reasons. It completes the loop-free half of the language on your own keyboard, so that the loop proof below is the only thing left standing between you and <i>every command in this language is local</i>. And it is the shortest place to see a locality proof in which the command does nothing whatsoever to the state: the entire content is choosing which hypothesis to apply, which makes the shape of the argument visible with none of the heap reasoning of Unit 25 on top of it.",
     setup:"Two rules conclude a run of <code>.ite b c₁ c₂</code>, so the inversion gives two branches, and they are mirror images. In each, the sub-run goes from the same state to the same state as the whole conditional, so the hypothesis applies at exactly the arguments you were given. The guard premise transfers with no work; the paragraph above says why, and the pitfall says what it looks like when you use it in the wrong branch.",
     goal:"theorem heapLocal_ite {b : BExpr} {c₁ c₂ : Cmd}\n    (h₁ : HeapLocal c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocal c₂) : HeapLocal (.ite b c₁ c₂) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded: given a frame disjoint from <code>h</code> and a run of <code>.ite b c₁ c₂</code> from <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to <code>s'</code>, produce the disjointness of <code>s'.heap</code> from the frame and a run of the same conditional on the combined heap with the two equations. The run you are given came from one of two rules, and which one it was decides which of <code>h₁</code> and <code>h₂</code> is any use.",
       "Split on the rule. In the branch where the guard was true, the conditional ran <code>c₁</code>, from the same starting state to the same finishing state; so the locality of <code>c₁</code> at this frame gives you every component you owe, about <code>c₁</code>. Turn a big run of <code>c₁</code> back into a big run of the conditional by applying the same rule again, with the same guard fact.",
       "<code>cases hex with | iteTrue hb hex' =&gt; … | iteFalse hb hex' =&gt; …</code>. In each branch, <code>obtain</code> the five components of the hypothesis applied to <code>hex'</code>, then close with <code>exact</code> and a five-slot tuple whose third component is <code>Exec.iteTrue</code> or <code>Exec.iteFalse</code> applied to <code>hb</code> and the big run.",
       "<code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code>, then <code>cases hex with</code>. The first branch is <code>| iteTrue hb hex' =&gt;</code> followed by <code>obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame s' hd hex'</code>, which leaves you holding all five components; the line after it is a single <code>exact</code>. The second branch is the same with <code>h₂</code> and <code>Exec.iteFalse</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem heapLocal_ite {b : BExpr} {c₁ c₂ : Cmd}\n    (h₁ : HeapLocal c₁) (h₂ : HeapLocal c₂) : HeapLocal (.ite b c₁ c₂) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | iteTrue hb hex' =>\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame s' hd hex'\n      exact ⟨hdEnd, r, Exec.iteTrue hb hr, hst, hhp⟩\n  | iteFalse hb hex' =>\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₂ σ h hFrame s' hd hex'\n      exact ⟨hdEnd, r, Exec.iteFalse hb hr, hst, hhp⟩",
     solNote:"Eight lines and no arithmetic. If the second branch does not compile after the first one does, the thing to check is which hypothesis and which rule you named.",
     expl:"A conditional does nothing; the command inside it does everything. Each branch of the inversion says which command that was, and the locality of that command, applied at the very arguments in scope, produces all five components. Rebuilding the big run means applying the same rule to the same guard fact, which crosses from the small heap to the big one by reduction rather than by proof.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex', h:"Discharged the binders of <code>HeapLocal</code>. The goal is the conjunction with the command <code>.ite b c₁ c₂</code>."},
       {tac:'cases hex with', h:"Inversion. Two rules conclude a run of a conditional, so two branches — and unlike the memory commands, neither of them tells you anything about <code>s'</code>."},
       {tac:'| iteTrue hb hex\' =>', h:"The branch where the guard evaluated to <code>true</code>. <code>hb</code> is that fact, stated about the small state; <code>hex'</code> is a run of <code>c₁</code> from <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to <code>s'</code> — the same endpoints as the conditional&rsquo;s own run."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame s\' hd hex\'', h:"Applied the locality of <code>c₁</code> at exactly the arguments in scope, and took the five components apart. <code>hdEnd</code> is the first half of the goal; <code>hst</code> and <code>hhp</code> are the last two, unchanged, because the conditional and its branch have the same final state."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨hdEnd, r, Exec.iteTrue hb hr, hst, hhp⟩', h:"Assembled. The witness is <code>r</code>, the state the big run of <code>c₁</code> reached, and <code>Exec.iteTrue hb hr</code> promotes that run to a run of the conditional. <code>hb</code>, a fact about <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code>, is accepted where a fact about <code>⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩</code> is expected."},
       {tac:'| iteFalse hb hex\' =>', h:"The mirror branch, with <code>hb</code> now saying the guard evaluated to <code>false</code>."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₂ σ h hFrame s\' hd hex\'', h:"The same application with the other hypothesis, because the run inside is a run of <code>c₂</code>."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨hdEnd, r, Exec.iteFalse hb hr, hst, hhp⟩', h:"The same assembly with the other rule."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'The iteTrue branch',
        start:"after intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex",
        steps:[
          {tac:'cases hex with | iteTrue hb hex\' =>',
           state:"case iteTrue\nhb : BExpr.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store b = true\nhex' : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h } s'\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec (Cmd.ite b c₁ c₂) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧\n        r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame",
           h:"Eight unchanged context lines above <code>hb</code> are omitted here and below. <code>hex'</code> runs from the same state to the same state as the conditional did, which is what makes the hypothesis applicable at the arguments already in scope."},
          {tac:'obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hr, hst, hhp⟩ := h₁ σ h hFrame s\' hd hex\'',
           state:"hdEnd : s'.heap.disjoint hFrame\nr : State\nhr : Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r\nhst : r.store = s'.store\nhhp : r.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame",
           h:"Five new lines, and four of them are components of the goal as they stand. Only <code>hr</code> needs work, and the work is one rule application: it is a run of <code>c₁</code> where a run of the conditional is wanted."}
        ],
        done:'exact ⟨hdEnd, r, Exec.iteTrue hb hr, hst, hhp⟩ — No goals.'},
       {t:'p', h:"There is no equation to prove anywhere in this theorem, and no <code>have</code>. The reason is visible in the first state above: <code>Exec.iteTrue</code> has a single sub-run, and its two endpoints are the conditional&rsquo;s two endpoints. Nothing is chained. Both of the other two constructors on this page chain — <code>Exec.seq</code> requires the first run to end where the second begins, and <code>Exec.loopTrue</code> requires the body to end where the rest of the loop begins — and in each case the run you have lifted ends at a state named by the existential, while the run you want to attach to it starts at a state written out by its fields. That is where the equation comes from, and it is why the loop proof below repeats these four lines almost verbatim. <code>ite</code> is the one of the three with no middle state to reconcile."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Naming the wrong rule in the rebuild, most often by writing <code>Exec.iteFalse</code> in both branches after copying the second one from the first. The error is an <code>Application type mismatch</code> on <code>hb</code>, and reading it is the lesson: it says <code>hb</code> has type <code>BExpr.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store b = true</code> but is expected to have type <code>BExpr.eval { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame }.store b = false</code>. The two heaps in that message are different and are <i>not</i> the disagreement — those two types reduce to the same thing. The disagreement is <code>true</code> against <code>false</code>. A reader who reads the message top to bottom and stops at the first difference will start trying to convert one heap into the other, and there is nothing to convert.",
     variants:"Apply <code>h₂</code> in the <code>iteTrue</code> branch and the failure moves to <code>hex'</code>: <code>Application type mismatch</code>, <code>hex'</code> has type <code>Exec c₁ { store := σ, heap := h } s'</code> but is expected to have type <code>Exec c₂ { store := σ, heap := h } s'</code>. No rearrangement helps, because which command ran is exactly what the branch is about. &middot; Give <code>BExpr.eval</code> the type <code>State → BExpr → Bool</code> instead of <code>Store → BExpr → Bool</code> and <code>hb</code> stops transferring: the two guard facts are then about genuinely different heaps and neither reduces to the other. The theorem does not become harder to prove; it becomes false, and the counterexample is the <code>guardAllocated</code> test above, which answers <code>false</code> on the empty heap and <code>true</code> once a frame supplies address 0. &middot; Delete the disjointness hypothesis from <code>HeapLocal</code>, keeping the conjunct, and these eight lines still compile: <code>hd</code> comes out of the <code>intro</code> and out of the two applications, and no tactic changes, because nothing here ever consulted it. That is <i>not</i> what happens to Unit 24&rsquo;s three. <code>heapLocal_skip</code> proves the conjunct by handing <code>hd</code> over as it stands, so with the hypothesis gone it reports <code>don't know how to synthesize placeholder for argument `left`</code> and leaves <code>⊢ { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.disjoint hFrame</code> with nothing in scope to close it. <code>ite</code> survives for the opposite reason to the one that makes those three easy: it never proves the conjunct at all, it forwards the one its sub-command proved."
    },

    /* ========================================================= loops === */

    {t:'sec', s:'Loops, and an induction the language refuses'},

    {t:'p', h:"Two rules conclude a run of <code>.loop b c</code>. <code>Exec.loopFalse</code> is the guard failing and the state unchanged, which is <code>skip</code> under another name. <code>Exec.loopTrue</code> has three premises: the guard succeeded, the body ran once, and then <i>the loop ran again</i> — and that third premise is a run of the very command being analysed. Inversion hands it to you and gives you no way to use it."},

    {t:'state', cap:"The <code>loopTrue</code> branch after <code>cases hex</code>, inside a proof of <code>HeapLocal (.loop b c)</code> opened the usual way, with the seven unchanged context lines above <code>s'✝</code> omitted. The hypothesis <code>hrest</code> is the theorem&rsquo;s own subject, one derivation shorter.",
     src:"case loopTrue\ns'✝ : State\nhb : BExpr.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store b = true\nhbody : Exec c { store := σ, heap := h } s'✝\nhrest : Exec (Cmd.loop b c) s'✝ s'\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec (Cmd.loop b c) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧ r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame"},

    {t:'p', h:"What is wanted for <code>hrest</code> is the conclusion of the theorem itself, at the middle state — and that is what an induction hypothesis is. Unit 20 built inductions over derivations for exactly this: <code>induction</code> on a derivation gives a branch per rule and, in each branch, the statement being proved assumed for every sub-derivation. So the move is to replace <code>cases</code> by <code>induction</code>."},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"The obvious repair.",
     src:"theorem loopBad {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd} (hc : HeapLocal c) :\n    HeapLocal (.loop b c) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  induction hex with\n  | loopFalse hb => sorry\n  | loopTrue hb hbody hrest ih1 ih2 => sorry"},

    {t:'state', cap:"Refused before any branch is looked at.",
     src:"error: Invalid target: Index in target's type is not a variable (consider using the `cases` tactic instead)\n  Cmd.loop b c"},

    {t:'p', h:"Unit 19 declared <code>Exec : Cmd → State → State → Prop</code>, and said that the three arguments are <i>indices</i>: they may differ from one constructor to another, which is what lets <code>Exec.assign</code> conclude something about one command and <code>Exec.free</code> about another. Induction over an inductively defined relation proves a statement for <i>every</i> value of the indices at once — that is what the induction hypotheses are hypotheses about — so the tactic has to abstract the indices, and it can only abstract a variable. Here the first index is the fixed term <code>Cmd.loop b c</code>. <code>cases</code> has no such requirement, because it takes one derivation apart one level and never has to speak about the others; that is exactly why the parenthesis in the message recommends it and why the recommendation is useless."},

    {t:'p', h:"There is a standard repair, and it costs one auxiliary theorem. Prove the statement for a <i>variable</i> command, and carry the fact that the command is the loop as an ordinary hypothesis."},

    {t:'steps', title:'The constant-command idiom',
     items:[
       {k:'Make the index a variable', h:"State the theorem as <code>∀ {cmd : Cmd} …, Exec cmd s s' → …</code>. Now the index is <code>cmd</code>, and <code>induction</code> has something it can abstract."},
       {k:'Carry the equation as a premise', h:"Add <code>cmd = .loop b₀ c₀ →</code> in front of the conclusion. Nothing has been weakened: the two together say what the original said, and the original is recovered by supplying <code>rfl</code>."},
       {k:'Induct', h:"Ten branches, one per rule. In each, the conclusion is guarded by an equation whose left-hand side is that rule&rsquo;s command, spelled out."},
       {k:'Kill the eight impossible branches', h:"In the <code>skip</code> branch the premise is <code>Cmd.skip = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀</code>. Distinct constructors of an inductive type are never equal, so <code>intro heq; cases heq</code> enumerates the ways a proof of it could have been built, finds none, and the branch is over — the same move that refuted <code>some v = none</code> in Unit 02. Seven more branches go the same way."},
       {k:'Read the equation in the two that survive', h:"In the <code>loopFalse</code> and <code>loopTrue</code> branches the equation is <code>Cmd.loop b✝ c✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀</code>, between the <i>same</i> constructor. There the same tactic does its other job, the one Unit 20 used on <code>some v = some v'</code>: constructor injectivity. It learns <code>b✝ = b₀</code> and <code>c✝ = c₀</code> and substitutes throughout, and the branch tag gains <code>.refl</code>."}
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"One more thing has to be arranged, and it is easy to get wrong because it is invisible in the statement of the original theorem. The induction hypothesis for <code>loopTrue</code> is about the rest of the loop, which starts at the <i>middle</i> state — so it must be a statement that can be applied to a disjointness fact about the middle heap, and the disjointness fact you were handed is about the starting heap. Leaving <code>∀ hFrame, Heap.disjoint s.heap hFrame →</code> inside the conclusion, to the right of the arrow, is what puts it into the statement the induction generalises. Unit 20 did the same thing with the tactic keyword <code>generalizing</code>; writing the quantifier into the statement and stopping <code>intro</code> short is the other way of doing it, and it is the way this proof takes."},

    {t:'anat', tag:'verified',
     src:"theorem heapLocal_loop_aux {b₀ : BExpr} {c₀ : Cmd} (hc : HeapLocal c₀) :\n    ∀ {cmd : Cmd} {s s' : State}, Exec cmd s s' → cmd = .loop b₀ c₀ →\n      ∀ hFrame, Heap.disjoint s.heap hFrame →\n        Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame ∧\n        ∃ r : State, Exec (.loop b₀ c₀) ⟨s.store, Heap.union s.heap hFrame⟩ r ∧\n          r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame := by",
     parts:[
       {m:'{b₀ : BExpr} {c₀ : Cmd}', h:"The guard and body of the loop under analysis, fixed for the whole theorem. They are named with subscript zero because <code>b</code> and <code>c</code> will be taken by the constructors during the induction, and a collision there is a proof that reads as though two different loops were involved."},
       {m:'∀ {cmd : Cmd} {s s\' : State}', h:"The three indices of <code>Exec</code>, quantified inside the statement rather than bound as parameters of the theorem. This is the whole repair: the first argument of <code>Exec</code> below is now a variable."},
       {m:'cmd = .loop b₀ c₀ →', h:"The premise that recovers what was given away. Everything the conclusion says is said only about derivations whose command is this loop; the other eight rules produce branches whose premise is false."},
       {m:'∀ hFrame, Heap.disjoint s.heap hFrame →', h:"Left to the right of the arrow on purpose. The induction abstracts everything from here on, so each induction hypothesis is available at whatever frame and whatever disjointness proof its own starting state needs — and the recursive branch needs one about a state that did not exist when the theorem was applied."},
       {m:'Exec (.loop b₀ c₀) ⟨s.store, Heap.union s.heap hFrame⟩ r', h:"The conclusion is <code>HeapLocal</code>&rsquo;s, written out with <code>s</code> and <code>s'</code> in place of <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> and the final state. It has to be written out rather than abbreviated, because <code>HeapLocal</code> quantifies over a store and a heap separately and here the starting state is a variable."}
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"After <code>intro cmd s s' hex</code> and the induction, a dead branch looks like this — the equation in front, everything else behind it."},

    {t:'state', cap:"The <code>skip</code> branch. The six further lines of the goal are the conclusion of the statement, word for word, and are omitted here; the whole thirteen-line display is in the fold below.",
     src:"case skip\nb₀ : BExpr\nc₀ : Cmd\nhc : HeapLocal c₀\ncmd : Cmd\ns s' s✝ : State\n⊢ Cmd.skip = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ →"},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>intro heq</code> takes the equation into the context and <code>cases heq</code> asks Lean to consider the ways it could hold. There are none, so the branch is finished, and the same two tactics finish seven others. Three states are named in that display: <code>s</code> and <code>s'</code> are the ones <code>intro</code> made, and <code>s✝</code> is the one the <code>skip</code> rule introduced. The goal is about <code>s✝</code> and not about either of the others because <code>induction</code> abstracted the two states the derivation was indexed by, and each branch got back whatever states its own rule uses. <code>s</code> and <code>s'</code> are still in scope and no longer say anything."},

    {t:'detail', title:'Two branches in full, including the one with two induction hypotheses', tag:'aside', open:false,
     blocks:[
       {t:'p', h:"The <code>skip</code> branch as Lean prints it. The conclusion under the arrow is the statement, unchanged; the whole content of the branch is its first line."},
       {t:'state', cap:"Thirteen lines.",
        src:"case skip\nb₀ : BExpr\nc₀ : Cmd\nhc : HeapLocal c₀\ncmd : Cmd\ns s' s✝ : State\n⊢ Cmd.skip = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ →\n    ∀ (hFrame : Heap),\n      s✝.heap.disjoint hFrame →\n        s✝.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n          ∃ r,\n            Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) { store := s✝.store, heap := s✝.heap.union hFrame } r ∧\n              r.store = s✝.store ∧ r.heap = s✝.heap.union hFrame"},
       {t:'p', h:"The <code>seq</code> branch is the one that shows what the induction is paying for. It carries two induction hypotheses, one per sub-derivation, each with its own equation premise — and both are useless, because the branch dies on <code>c₁✝ ;; c₂✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀</code> before either can be reached. Eight of the ten branches die on their equation like this, and three of the eight carry induction hypotheses they never reach. The tactic builds them whether or not the branch can happen."},
       {t:'state', cap:"The <code>seq</code> branch. This is the reason the corpus proof writes <code>| seq _ _ _ _ =&gt;</code> with four underscores: two premises and two induction hypotheses.",
        src:"case seq\nb₀ : BExpr\nc₀ : Cmd\nhc : HeapLocal c₀\ncmd : Cmd\ns s' s✝ s'✝ s''✝ : State\nc₁✝ c₂✝ : Cmd\nh₁✝ : Exec c₁✝ s✝ s'✝\nh₂✝ : Exec c₂✝ s'✝ s''✝\nh₁_ih✝ :\n  c₁✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ →\n    ∀ (hFrame : Heap),\n      s✝.heap.disjoint hFrame →\n        s'✝.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n          ∃ r,\n            Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) { store := s✝.store, heap := s✝.heap.union hFrame } r ∧\n              r.store = s'✝.store ∧ r.heap = s'✝.heap.union hFrame\nh₂_ih✝ :\n  c₂✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ →\n    ∀ (hFrame : Heap),\n      s'✝.heap.disjoint hFrame →\n        s''✝.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n          ∃ r,\n            Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) { store := s'✝.store, heap := s'✝.heap.union hFrame } r ∧\n              r.store = s''✝.store ∧ r.heap = s''✝.heap.union hFrame\n⊢ c₁✝ ;; c₂✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ →\n    ∀ (hFrame : Heap),\n      s✝.heap.disjoint hFrame →\n        s''✝.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n          ∃ r,\n            Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) { store := s✝.store, heap := s✝.heap.union hFrame } r ∧\n              r.store = s''✝.store ∧ r.heap = s''✝.heap.union hFrame"},
       {t:'p', h:"Providing more names than an alternative has is the error the sequencing section met. Providing fewer is allowed and silent: <code>| seq _ _ =&gt;</code> compiles, and the two induction hypotheses stay inaccessible. That is the failure to watch for, because under <code>induction</code> the alternatives are longer than the constructors they are named after, and a name list copied from a <code>cases</code> proof will be short by exactly the number of recursive premises."}
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"The branch that has the work in it is <code>loopTrue</code>, and the shape of its induction hypothesis is the thing to look at before writing anything."},

    {t:'state', cap:"One hypothesis, extracted from the <code>loopTrue</code> context. The quantifier over the frame, and the disjointness premise under it, are there because the statement left them to the right of the arrow.",
     src:"ihrest :\n  Cmd.loop b✝ c✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ →\n    ∀ (hFrame : Heap),\n      s'✝.heap.disjoint hFrame →\n        s''✝.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n          ∃ r,\n            Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) { store := s'✝.store, heap := s'✝.heap.union hFrame } r ∧\n              r.store = s''✝.store ∧ r.heap = s''✝.heap.union hFrame"},

    {t:'p', h:"Three things to read off it. Its equation premise is between the same constructor on both sides, so it is discharged by <code>rfl</code> rather than refuted. Its disjointness premise is about <code>s'✝</code>, the state after one pass of the body — which is a heap you do not have a disjointness proof for until you have applied <code>hc</code>, the locality of the body, to that pass. And its conclusion is the conclusion of the whole theorem for the tail of the loop, which is what makes the last step of the branch a repeat of the sequencing argument: a body run and a rest run, glued by <code>Exec.loopTrue</code>, with the same equation between states standing between the state one produced and the state the other consumes."},

    {t:'p', h:"That leaves <code>loopFalse</code>, where the loop stopped without doing anything: the final state is the starting state, the disjointness you were handed is the disjointness you owe, and the big run is <code>Exec.loopFalse</code> applied to the same guard fact, which transfers for the reason the conditional&rsquo;s guard transferred. Both equations are <code>rfl</code>."},

    {t:'p', h:"With the auxiliary theorem proved, the theorem itself is two lines: open <code>HeapLocal</code>, and apply the auxiliary at the derivation you were given, with <code>rfl</code> for the equation and the frame and disjointness passed straight through. The starting state there is the literal <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code>, and everything the auxiliary says about <code>s.store</code> and <code>s.heap</code> reduces to something about <code>σ</code> and <code>h</code> without a word being said."},

    /* =================================================== exercise x53 === */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x53',
     name:'heapLocal_loop',
     hard:true,
     why:"The course&rsquo;s second induction over a derivation, and the first where the statement has to be strengthened before the induction will produce anything usable. Both parts of that — a variable index with an equation carried alongside it, and a hypothesis deliberately left under a quantifier — are needed again in Unit 36, on the invariant rule, where the statement being strengthened is one the reader chooses rather than one handed over. This is also the theorem that finishes the language: with it, all eight ways of building a command are known to preserve locality, and the frame rule Unit 27 proves applies to programs.",
     setup:"You are asked for two declarations. The auxiliary one has the statement below, which is the constant-command idiom applied to <code>HeapLocal (.loop b₀ c₀)</code> — read the five callouts on it above before starting. Then <code>heapLocal_loop</code> follows from it in two lines. In the <code>loopTrue</code> branch the states arrive inaccessible, as they did in the sequencing proof, and <code>rename_i s s' s'' _ _</code> names the three of them; the two trailing underscores are for the body&rsquo;s induction hypothesis and the rest-run premise, which are inaccessible too and stay that way. The last four lines of that branch are the sequencing proof again.",
     goal:"theorem heapLocal_loop_aux {b₀ : BExpr} {c₀ : Cmd} (hc : HeapLocal c₀) :\n    ∀ {cmd : Cmd} {s s' : State}, Exec cmd s s' → cmd = .loop b₀ c₀ →\n      ∀ hFrame, Heap.disjoint s.heap hFrame →\n        Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame ∧\n        ∃ r : State, Exec (.loop b₀ c₀) ⟨s.store, Heap.union s.heap hFrame⟩ r ∧\n          r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame := by\n  sorry\n\ntheorem heapLocal_loop {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd} (hc : HeapLocal c) :\n    HeapLocal (.loop b c) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, the auxiliary says: for any command, any two states and any derivation of a run of that command between them, <i>if</i> that command happens to be <code>.loop b₀ c₀</code>, then for every frame disjoint from the starting heap, the finishing heap is still disjoint from that frame, and the same loop run on the combined heap reaches a state whose store is the finishing store and whose heap is the finishing heap with the frame beside it. The equation is a premise, so nothing has been given away: hand it <code>rfl</code> and what comes back is <code>HeapLocal (.loop b₀ c₀)</code>, which is the second theorem.",
       "Induct on the derivation. Ten branches; in eight of them the equation premise says one constructor of <code>Cmd</code> equals another, which cannot happen, so the branch closes with no work. In <code>loopFalse</code> nothing ran, so the state you must exhibit is the one you started on. In <code>loopTrue</code> you have a run of the body and, for the rest of the loop, the statement being proved; apply the locality of the body to the first, feed the disjointness that produces to the second, and join the two lifted runs with the rule that produced this branch. That last move decides what the statement must quantify over: the second application happens at the heap the body <i>left</i>, not at the one the theorem was handed, so the frame and its disjointness have to be part of what the induction generalises rather than facts fixed before it runs.",
       "Stop <code>intro</code> after the derivation, so that the equation, the frame and the disjointness are all still in the goal when the induction runs. Then <code>induction hex with</code>, and in each dead branch <code>intro heq; cases heq</code>. In the two live branches, <code>intro heq hFrame hd</code> first and then <code>cases heq</code>, which here substitutes rather than refutes. Use <code>rename_i</code> for the states in <code>loopTrue</code>, <code>obtain</code> for both applications, and a <code>have</code> with two backwards rewrites for the state equation, exactly as in <code>heapLocal_seq</code>. The final theorem is <code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code> and one <code>exact</code>.",
       "<code>intro cmd s s' hex</code> then <code>induction hex with</code>. The <code>skip</code> line is <code>| skip =&gt; intro heq; cases heq</code>, and <code>assign</code>, <code>load _</code>, <code>write _</code>, <code>free _</code>, <code>seq _ _ _ _</code>, <code>iteTrue _ _ _</code>, <code>iteFalse _ _ _</code> take the same body — the underscores count each alternative&rsquo;s premises and induction hypotheses. <code>| loopFalse hb =&gt;</code> is <code>intro heq hFrame hd</code>, <code>cases heq</code>, then <code>exact ⟨hd, _, Exec.loopFalse hb, rfl, rfl⟩</code>. <code>| loopTrue hb hbody _ _ ihrest =&gt;</code> opens the same way, then <code>rename_i s s' s'' _ _</code>, then <code>obtain … := hc s.store s.heap hFrame s' hd hbody</code> and <code>obtain … := ihrest rfl hFrame hdMid</code>. The last theorem is <code>exact heapLocal_loop_aux hc hex rfl hFrame hd</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem heapLocal_loop_aux {b₀ : BExpr} {c₀ : Cmd} (hc : HeapLocal c₀) :\n    ∀ {cmd : Cmd} {s s' : State}, Exec cmd s s' → cmd = .loop b₀ c₀ →\n      ∀ hFrame, Heap.disjoint s.heap hFrame →\n        Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame ∧\n        ∃ r : State, Exec (.loop b₀ c₀) ⟨s.store, Heap.union s.heap hFrame⟩ r ∧\n          r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame := by\n  intro cmd s s' hex\n  induction hex with\n  | skip => intro heq; cases heq\n  | assign => intro heq; cases heq\n  | load _ => intro heq; cases heq\n  | write _ => intro heq; cases heq\n  | free _ => intro heq; cases heq\n  | seq _ _ _ _ => intro heq; cases heq\n  | iteTrue _ _ _ => intro heq; cases heq\n  | iteFalse _ _ _ => intro heq; cases heq\n  | loopFalse hb =>\n      intro heq hFrame hd\n      cases heq\n      exact ⟨hd, _, Exec.loopFalse hb, rfl, rfl⟩\n  | loopTrue hb hbody _ _ ihrest =>\n      intro heq hFrame hd\n      cases heq\n      rename_i s s' s'' _ _\n      obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := hc s.store s.heap hFrame s' hd hbody\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := ihrest rfl hFrame hdMid\n      have heq2 : r₁ = ⟨s'.store, Heap.union s'.heap hFrame⟩ := by\n        rw [← hst₁, ← hhp₁]\n      refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.loopTrue (s' := r₁) hb hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩\n      rw [heq2]\n      exact hr₂\n\ntheorem heapLocal_loop {b : BExpr} {c : Cmd} (hc : HeapLocal c) :\n    HeapLocal (.loop b c) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  exact heapLocal_loop_aux hc hex rfl hFrame hd",
     solNote:"Thirty-five lines, of which eight are one line written eight times and the whole <code>loopTrue</code> branch is the sequencing proof with the names changed. The new material is the six-line statement and the four lines that open that branch. If you climbed all four hints and are still stuck, open this and read the statement first; the proof follows from it more or less mechanically once the shape of the induction hypothesis is clear.",
     expl:"An induction over the derivation, made possible by replacing the fixed command with a variable and carrying the equation that says what it is. Eight branches are then unreachable and say so; <code>loopFalse</code> is the identity case; <code>loopTrue</code> is <code>heapLocal_seq</code> with the second hypothesis replaced by the induction hypothesis. The disjointness lives to the right of the arrow so that the induction hypothesis can be applied at the middle heap rather than at the one the theorem started from.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro cmd s s\' hex', h:"Stopped exactly here. The equation, the frame and the disjointness stay in the goal, which is what makes them part of what the induction generalises."},
       {tac:'induction hex with', h:"Ten branches, one per constructor of <code>Exec</code>, each with an induction hypothesis per sub-derivation. In every branch the goal is an implication whose premise is an equation between <code>Cmd</code>s."},
       {tac:'| skip => intro heq; cases heq', h:"The premise is <code>Cmd.skip = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀</code>. <code>cases</code> enumerates the ways a proof of it could have been built; there are none, so the branch is discharged without a goal ever being addressed."},
       {tac:'| assign => intro heq; cases heq', h:"The same, with <code>Cmd.assign x✝ e✝</code> on the left. <code>Exec.assign</code> has no premises, so the alternative takes no names."},
       {tac:'| load _ => intro heq; cases heq', h:"One underscore, for <code>Exec.load</code>&rsquo;s single premise. The underscore declines to name it rather than omitting it; the branch never looks at it."},
       {tac:'| write _ => intro heq; cases heq', h:"The same shape, and <code>| free _ =&gt;</code> is the identical line. Both rules carry one premise about the heap, and neither is consulted."},
       {tac:'| seq _ _ _ _ => intro heq; cases heq', h:"Four underscores: two premises and the two induction hypotheses the tactic generated for them. Both hypotheses are unusable and unused — the branch dies on <code>c₁✝ ;; c₂✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀</code>."},
       {tac:'| iteTrue _ _ _ => intro heq; cases heq', h:"Three: the guard, the sub-run, and one induction hypothesis. <code>| iteFalse _ _ _ =&gt;</code> is the same."},
       {tac:'| loopFalse hb =>', h:"The first branch that can happen. The guard is named because the rebuild needs it."},
       {tac:'intro heq hFrame hd', h:"Took the three remaining premises of the goal into the context: the equation, the frame, and the disjointness of the starting heap from it. Only now is the goal the conjunction itself."},
       {tac:'cases heq', h:"Here the equation is between two <code>Cmd.loop</code>s, so instead of refuting it Lean takes it apart: <code>b✝</code> becomes <code>b₀</code> and <code>c✝</code> becomes <code>c₀</code> everywhere, including in <code>hb</code>. The branch tag becomes <code>loopFalse.refl</code>."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨hd, _, Exec.loopFalse hb, rfl, rfl⟩', h:"Nothing ran, so the finishing state is the starting state and <code>hd</code> is already the first component. The witness is left as <code>_</code> for Lean to read off <code>Exec.loopFalse</code>, whose conclusion fixes the final state to be the initial one; both equations are then <code>rfl</code>. The guard fact crosses from the small heap to the big one by reduction, exactly as in <code>heapLocal_ite</code>."},
       {tac:'| loopTrue hb hbody _ _ ihrest =>', h:"Five names for three premises and two induction hypotheses, premises first: guard, body run, rest run, then the hypothesis about the body and the hypothesis about the rest. The two declined are the third and the fourth. The rest-run premise is declined because <code>ihrest</code> says everything about that run this proof needs; the body&rsquo;s hypothesis is declined because it is dead — it is guarded by <code>c₀ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀</code>, an equation nobody can supply, and the body&rsquo;s locality comes from <code>hc</code> instead."},
       {tac:'intro heq hFrame hd', h:"As in the other live branch."},
       {tac:'cases heq', h:"Substituted <code>b₀</code> and <code>c₀</code> for the constructor&rsquo;s own guard and body, which is what turns <code>hbody</code> into a run of <code>c₀</code> — the command <code>hc</code> is about — and <code>ihrest</code>&rsquo;s premise into <code>Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀</code>."},
       {tac:'rename_i s s\' s\'\' _ _', h:"Named the three states the rule introduced: before the body, after the body, after the rest. The two underscores keep the body&rsquo;s induction hypothesis and the rest-run premise inaccessible. The names <code>s</code> and <code>s'</code> shadow the two that <code>intro</code> made at the top, which are no longer of any use in this branch."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := hc s.store s.heap hFrame s\' hd hbody', h:"The locality of the body, applied to one pass. It produces the disjointness of the middle heap — the fact the induction hypothesis is waiting for — and a big run of the body ending at <code>r₁</code>, described by its two fields."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := ihrest rfl hFrame hdMid', h:"The induction hypothesis, applied to the tail of the loop: <code>rfl</code> discharges its equation, then the frame, then <code>hdMid</code>. Three of its five components are components of the goal as they stand."},
       {tac:'have heq2 : r₁ = ⟨s\'.store, Heap.union s\'.heap hFrame⟩ := by rw [← hst₁, ← hhp₁]', h:"The same identification as in <code>heapLocal_seq</code>, for the same reason: the big body run ends at <code>r₁</code> and the big tail run starts at a state written out by its fields."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.loopTrue (s\' := r₁) hb hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩', h:"Assembled, with the loop rule joining the big body run to a hole for the big tail run. The named argument fixes the rule&rsquo;s middle state to <code>r₁</code>; Lean would find it from <code>hr₁</code> anyway, and writing it makes the type of the hole depend on nothing but what is on this line."},
       {tac:'rw [heq2]; exact hr₂', h:"Replaced <code>r₁</code> by its fields in the goal, which turns it into <code>hr₂</code>&rsquo;s type."},
       {tac:'theorem heapLocal_loop … intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex', h:"The theorem itself. Opening <code>HeapLocal</code> gives a starting state that is the literal <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code>."},
       {tac:'exact heapLocal_loop_aux hc hex rfl hFrame hd', h:"The auxiliary at the derivation in hand. <code>rfl</code> supplies the equation, which is where the strengthening is paid back; <code>hFrame</code> and <code>hd</code> go straight through. Everything the auxiliary says about <code>s.store</code> and <code>s.heap</code> is about <code>⟨σ, h⟩.store</code> and <code>⟨σ, h⟩.heap</code> here, and those reduce to <code>σ</code> and <code>h</code> with no tactic."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'The loopTrue branch, from the induction to the last hole',
        start:"the loopTrue branch, immediately after induction hex with",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro heq hFrame hd',
           state:"heq : Cmd.loop b✝ c✝ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀\nhFrame : Heap\nhd : s✝.heap.disjoint hFrame",
           h:"Only new context lines are shown at each step; the display in full runs to thirty lines, of which sixteen are the branch&rsquo;s two induction hypotheses. The one this proof uses is printed above, on the main line. The equation is between two <code>Cmd.loop</code>s, so this branch is one of the two that survive."},
          {tac:'cases heq',
           state:"case loopTrue.refl\nhb : BExpr.eval s✝.store b₀ = true\nhbody : Exec c₀ s✝ s'✝\nihrest :\n  Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ = Cmd.loop b₀ c₀ →\n    ∀ (hFrame : Heap),\n      s'✝.heap.disjoint hFrame →\n        s''✝.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n          ∃ r,\n            Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) { store := s'✝.store, heap := s'✝.heap.union hFrame } r ∧\n              r.store = s''✝.store ∧ r.heap = s''✝.heap.union hFrame",
           h:"Injectivity, not refutation: <code>b✝</code> and <code>c✝</code> are replaced by <code>b₀</code> and <code>c₀</code> everywhere. <code>hbody</code> is now a run of <code>c₀</code>, which is the command <code>hc</code> is about, and <code>ihrest</code>&rsquo;s premise has become an identity. The tag records the equality proof that was consumed."},
          {tac:'rename_i s s\' s\'\' _ _',
           state:"s✝ s'✝ s s' s'' : State\nhd : s.heap.disjoint hFrame\nhb : BExpr.eval s.store b₀ = true\nhbody : Exec c₀ s s'",
           h:"The three states of this branch get names, and the two the theorem introduced at the top are pushed into inaccessibility under the same spellings. From here the branch reads like the sequencing proof: a run of <code>c₀</code> from <code>s</code> to <code>s'</code>, and a run of the loop from <code>s'</code> to <code>s''</code>."},
          {tac:'obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := hc s.store s.heap hFrame s\' hd hbody',
           state:"hdMid : s'.heap.disjoint hFrame\nr₁ : State\nhr₁ : Exec c₀ { store := s.store, heap := s.heap.union hFrame } r₁\nhst₁ : r₁.store = s'.store\nhhp₁ : r₁.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame",
           h:"One pass of the body, run on the big heap. <code>hdMid</code> is what makes the next line possible, and it is the only thing in the branch that the induction hypothesis could not have produced for itself."},
          {tac:'obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := ihrest rfl hFrame hdMid',
           state:"hdEnd : s''.heap.disjoint hFrame\nr₂ : State\nhr₂ : Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) { store := s'.store, heap := s'.heap.union hFrame } r₂\nhst₂ : r₂.store = s''.store\nhhp₂ : r₂.heap = s''.heap.union hFrame",
           h:"The rest of the loop, on the big heap. Three of these five are components of the goal unchanged. Had the disjointness been introduced before the induction, this application would have had nothing to say about <code>s'</code>."},
          {tac:'refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.loopTrue (s\' := r₁) hb hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩',
           state:"case loopTrue.refl\n⊢ Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) r₁ r₂",
           h:"The same hole as in <code>heapLocal_seq</code>, with the loop rule in place of the sequencing rule, and closed by the same <code>have</code> and the same rewrite."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'},
       {t:'detail', title:'The variant that introduces everything before the induction', tag:'aside', open:false,
        blocks:[
          {t:'p', h:"The solution stops <code>intro</code> after the derivation, so the equation, the frame and the disjointness are introduced separately in each branch — two extra tokens per branch, ten branches. Introducing all of them before the induction also compiles, and the proof is shorter."},
          {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"The same theorem, the same statement, with <code>intro cmd s s' hex heq hFrame hd</code> in front of the induction and the per-branch <code>intro</code>s deleted. Everything else is unchanged except that <code>ihrest</code> now takes one argument fewer.",
           src:"theorem heapLocal_loop_aux_flat {b₀ : BExpr} {c₀ : Cmd} (hc : HeapLocal c₀) :\n    ∀ {cmd : Cmd} {s s' : State}, Exec cmd s s' → cmd = .loop b₀ c₀ →\n      ∀ hFrame, Heap.disjoint s.heap hFrame →\n        Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame ∧\n        ∃ r : State, Exec (.loop b₀ c₀) ⟨s.store, Heap.union s.heap hFrame⟩ r ∧\n          r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame := by\n  intro cmd s s' hex heq hFrame hd\n  induction hex with\n  | skip => cases heq\n  | assign => cases heq\n  | load _ => cases heq\n  | write _ => cases heq\n  | free _ => cases heq\n  | seq _ _ _ _ => cases heq\n  | iteTrue _ _ _ => cases heq\n  | iteFalse _ _ _ => cases heq\n  | loopFalse hb =>\n      cases heq\n      exact ⟨hd, _, Exec.loopFalse hb, rfl, rfl⟩\n  | loopTrue hb hbody _ _ ihrest =>\n      cases heq\n      rename_i s s' s'' _ _\n      obtain ⟨hdMid, r₁, hr₁, hst₁, hhp₁⟩ := hc s.store s.heap hFrame s' hd hbody\n      obtain ⟨hdEnd, r₂, hr₂, hst₂, hhp₂⟩ := ihrest rfl hdMid\n      have heq2 : r₁ = ⟨s'.store, Heap.union s'.heap hFrame⟩ := by\n        rw [← hst₁, ← hhp₁]\n      refine ⟨hdEnd, r₂, Exec.loopTrue (s' := r₁) hb hr₁ ?_, hst₂, hhp₂⟩\n      rw [heq2]\n      exact hr₂"},
          {t:'p', h:"It works because <code>induction</code> reverts, on its own, every hypothesis whose type mentions the thing being abstracted. <code>heq</code> mentions <code>cmd</code> and <code>hd</code> mentions <code>s</code>, so both are put back under the arrow before the branches are generated and reintroduced afterwards — which is why <code>ihrest</code> still quantifies over the disjointness and why the proof still works. <code>hFrame</code> is not reverted, because its type is <code>Heap</code> and mentions nothing; that costs nothing here only because the frame never changes."},
          {t:'p', h:"What the corpus version buys for its extra twenty tokens is that the generalisation is something you wrote rather than something the tactic did while you were not looking. The failure mode of the short version is a hypothesis that <i>does not</i> mention the induction target, is therefore not reverted, and quietly fixes a value the induction hypothesis needed to vary over — and the symptom is an induction hypothesis that will not apply, three lines later, for no visible reason. Unit 36 runs this idiom again on a harder statement, where the thing to generalise is a loop invariant."}
        ]},
       {t:'p', h:"The eight dead branches are not padding and cannot be replaced by a wildcard that also covers the live ones: <code>| _ =&gt; intro heq; cases heq</code> would be applied to <code>loopFalse</code> and <code>loopTrue</code> too, where <code>cases heq</code> succeeds without closing anything and leaves two goals no one has written a proof for. What can be shortened is the eight themselves; whether that is an improvement is a question about how much a reader should be told that a case is impossible."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Writing <code>| loopTrue hb hbody hrest =&gt;</code> — three names, one per premise, which is what the rule has and what every <code>cases</code> so far has wanted. Lean accepts it, because providing fewer names than an alternative offers is legal. The two induction hypotheses are then inaccessible, and the complaint arrives five lines later as <code>Unknown identifier `ihrest`</code>, pointing at the line that tries to use the hypothesis rather than at the line that failed to name it. A second message follows from the same line — <code>Tactic `rcases` failed: `x✝ : ?m.354` is not an inductive datatype</code> — which is the <code>obtain</code> complaining that it has been asked to take apart a term whose type it could not work out, because the term does not exist; it is noise, and chasing it leads nowhere. Under <code>induction</code> an alternative takes one name per premise <i>and</i> one per induction hypothesis, all the premises first: guard, body run, rest run, body hypothesis, rest hypothesis. Count them off the goal display rather than off the constructor.",
     variants:"Drop the equation device and induct on <code>hex : Exec (.loop b c) ⟨σ, h⟩ s'</code> directly, and no branch is ever generated: <code>Invalid target: Index in target's type is not a variable</code>, naming <code>Cmd.loop b c</code>. &middot; Keep the device but replace <code>induction</code> by <code>cases</code>, and the first thing you see is four copies of <code>Too many variable names provided at alternative</code>: <code>seq</code> now wants two names and not four, <code>iteTrue</code> and <code>iteFalse</code> two and not three, <code>loopTrue</code> three and not five — the surplus names were the induction hypotheses, and there are none. Shorten every list and it compiles as far as the branches: the equation still kills eight of them, and <code>loopTrue</code> is left holding <code>hrest : Exec (Cmd.loop b₀ c₀) s'✝ s'</code> with nothing to apply to it. That is the state the section opened on, reached with the whole idiom already paid for. &middot; Introduce the equation, the frame and the disjointness before the induction and the proof still compiles, because <code>induction</code> reverts every hypothesis mentioning the state or the command it is abstracting and reintroduces it in each branch; the fold in this panel has that version in full. The generalisation is then the tactic&rsquo;s decision rather than the statement&rsquo;s, and a hypothesis that happens not to mention the target — a fixed frame, say, in a proof where the frame changed — would silently not be generalised and the induction hypothesis would be too weak with nothing on the screen saying why."
    },

    /* ============================================================ close === */

    {t:'sec', s:'The language is finished'},

    {t:'p', h:"Eight constructors, eight theorems, three units. Five of them are about commands that touch memory and were proved one address at a time; three are about commands that contain other commands and were proved from the locality of what they contain, without ever asking what those commands do."},

    {t:'tbl', cap:"Every way of building a command, and where its locality was proved. The first five are settled by the one rule that can have fired; the last three are the closure properties that make the collection complete.",
     head:['command', 'theorem', 'unit', 'what the proof consults'],
     rows:[
       ['<code>skip</code>', '<code>heapLocal_skip</code>', '24', 'nothing'],
       ['<code>x := e</code>', '<code>heapLocal_assign</code>', '24', 'nothing'],
       ['<code>x := [l]</code>', '<code>heapLocal_load</code>', '24', 'the rule&rsquo;s premise, through <code>union_of_some</code>'],
       ['<code>[l] := e</code>', '<code>heapLocal_write</code>', '25', 'the premise and the disjointness, both in the disjointness half'],
       ['<code>free(l)</code>', '<code>heapLocal_free</code>', '25', 'the premise and the disjointness, both in the equation half'],
       ['<code>c₁ ;; c₂</code>', '<code>heapLocal_seq</code>', '26', 'locality of both halves; the disjointness is <i>spent</i> here'],
       ['<code>if b then c₁ else c₂</code>', '<code>heapLocal_ite</code>', '26', 'locality of one half; nothing about the heap'],
       ['<code>while b do c</code>', '<code>heapLocal_loop</code>', '26', 'locality of the body, once per iteration']
     ]},

    {t:'note', kind:'key', title:'What "every command is local" is worth',
     h:"The eight theorems are eight separate facts, and the last three are what make them one. A property proved of the five primitive commands says nothing about programs; a property proved of the primitives <i>and</i> closed under the three ways of combining them holds of every command the grammar can produce, by induction on that grammar — which is why nobody will ever have to do this again for a particular program. It also fixes the price of extending the language: one new constructor costs one new locality proof and no revision of any of these, because none of the three closure proofs looks at what its sub-commands are. Unit 38 spends that observation in the opposite direction, on a command whose locality proof does not exist."},

    {t:'dod', h:"You can prove that a property of runs is closed under sequencing by applying it twice and joining the two derivations, and say which conjunct of the property makes the second application legal. You can name an inaccessible variable with <code>rename_i</code> and say why the alternative&rsquo;s name list could not have named it. You can recognise the gap between a state a hypothesis produced and the pair of its fields, and close it with two backwards rewrites and structure eta. You can read <code>Invalid target: Index in target&rsquo;s type is not a variable</code> as a statement about what <code>induction</code> must abstract, and repair it by replacing the index with a variable and carrying an equation. You can decide which hypotheses to leave to the right of the arrow so that the induction hypothesis is strong enough, and say what goes wrong when one of them is introduced too early."},

    {t:'p', h:"Every command is local. That is a fact about the language. Turning it into a fact about triples is one theorem, eight lines, and every one of them is a step of the informal argument."}

  ]
});
